Understood, thanks. Your answers here settle everything I had, so I
have no questions left. v3 is posted as "[PATCH v3]
jails/instances/lab-mail: Initial spec" [1]. This reply only answers
your questions.

> Just to clarify - we'll be able to add more email addresses over time,
> correct?

Yes. The allowed senders are one setting in smtprelay's config
(allowed_sender, a regular expression). Adding an address is a
one-line change to the instance; the spec says so.

> Pick a non-standard port for it as well.

Dovecot now listens on 127.0.0.1:2143. The spec now says that neither
daemon uses a standard port (25, 587, 143, or 993).

> I assume we can also filter the destination addresses? That's a key
> design constraint.

Yes. smtprelay checks each RCPT TO against its allowed_recipients
setting, before it contacts Fastmail. A recipient outside
pat@patmaddox.com, @p7x.dev, and @lists.p7x.dev gets a 5xx reply in
the session. Step 5 of the acceptance test checks this.

> You say "lab-mail checks", but lab-mail is a host - what is the actual
> process name? e.g. "lab-mail's smtprelay checks..." if that's indeed the case.

It is smtprelay, as you guessed. v3 names the daemon that does each
job: smtprelay sends and filters, mbsync pulls from Fastmail, and
Dovecot serves IMAP. "lab-mail" is left only where the spec means the
jail.

> I don't think there's any value in two separate password. I only have
> one app password for the relay.

Done. The spec uses one app password, with IMAP and SMTP access,
shared by smtprelay and mbsync.

> That's outside the scope of the spec. claude@p7x.dev is its own email
> address, configurable however I feel like it.

Done. The spec lists where Fastmail delivers claude@p7x.dev's mail
under out of scope.

